It is note worthy that, the output doesn't provide any 'Debug.Assert()' statement for 'int Old_x' as it's value is neither checked for equality nor being checked in any 'Debug.Assert()'.--------------------This appears to be a testing framework code which uses Nunit for its testing. Nunit provides a way to assert the behavior of tests, known as Assert statements. However, the "PUT_GetContract", "PUT_ContainsContract", "PUT_IndexOfContract", "PUT_LastIndexOfContract", and "PUT_CountContract" methods are not using Assert statements directly to check the conditions you want to validate.

Instead, they perform basic operations like `IndexOf`, `Contains`, `Count` etc and assign the results to variables like `New_Ret`, which are then used in different tests to check the behavior. They also use `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` to output parameters that help you verify the test behavior after execution.

Here's how Assert statements might be used in place of these non-assert behavior checks:

```cs
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_GetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_index = index;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(index);

    int New_Ret = (int)(arrList[index]);

    Assert.AreEqual(arrList[index], New_Ret, $"Failed at index {index}");

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", arrList.Count);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_index", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

    Assert.AreEqual(arrList.IndexOf(x) > -1, New_Ret, $"The expected value '{x}' was not found in the array.");

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$Old_arrListLastIndexOfX", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_arrListLastIndexOfX", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
}
```

These changes use NUnit's Assert class to conditionally fail tests based on boolean expressions, which may be closer to how you intend to validate the test conditions. Also note that the changes are made only on the `PUT_GetContract`, `PUT_ContainsContract` methods because the methods used for these conditions don't make sense with what you intended to validate.

It's important to note that this translation requires assumptions and could fail due to some conditions not being respected. If it starts failing, the issue is likely due to other pieces of code not matching what you expect for the specific test case you're validating.--------------------```cs
using NUnit.Framework;
using System.Collections;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture]
    public class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [Test]
        public void PUT_AddContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            Assert.DoesNotThrow(() => arrList.Add(x));
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
        }

        [Test]
        public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            Assert.DoesNotThrow(() => arrList.Remove(x));
            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            Assert.Contains(x, arrList);
            arrList.Add(x);
            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount - 1, New_arrListCount);
        }

        [Test]
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            if (index <= arrList.Count && index >= 0)
            {
                arrList.Insert(index, x);
                Assert.Contains(x, arrList);
                Assert.Catch(() => arrList.Insert(index, x));
            }
            else
            {
                Assert.Catch(() => arrList.Insert(index, x));
            }
        }

        [Test]
        public void PUT_SetContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            if (index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count)
            {
                int old_number = (int)arrList[index];
                arrList[index] = x;
                Assert.AreNotEqual(x, old_number);
            }
            else
            {
                Assert.Catch(() => arrList[index] = x);
            }
        }

        [Test]
        public void PUT_GetContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            if (index < arrList.Count && index >= 0)
            {
                Assert.DoesNotThrow(() => { int ret = (int)arrList[index]; });
            }
            else
            {
                Assert.Null(() => { int ret = (int)arrList[index]; });
            }
        }

        [Test]
        public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            Assert.DoesNotThrow(() => arrList.Contains(x));
        }

        [Test]
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            Assert.DoesNotThrow(() => arrList.IndexOf(x));
        }

        [Test]
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            Assert.DoesNotThrow(() => arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
        }

        [Test]
        public void PUT_CountContract([PexPositiveTest, PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            Assert.DoesNotThrow(() => arrList.Count);
        }
    }
}
```

For the `"Arrange, Act, Assert"` pattern, the `[Arrange]` portion should be inside the testcase methods. I have refactored the code to fit this pattern. Please note, that the "Act" part in the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern corresponds to the lines where the ArrayList's `Count` property is modified, and the `Assert` part verifies the assertion.

In this code, only the "Assert" part is performed - you need to ensure that the corresponding "Act" part is performed correctly. In general, the "Act" part is the place where you call the methods to be tested.

